      "detail": {
        "type": "comment",
        "comment": {
          "forge": "forgejo",
          "instance": "codeberg.org",
          "thread": {
            "forge": "forgejo",
            "instance": "codeberg.org",
            "repo": {
              "forge": "forgejo",
              "instance": "codeberg.org",
              "owner": "forgejo",
              "name": "forgejo",
              "externalId": "73144"
            },
            "kind": "issue",
            "number": "14178"
          },
          "id": "28372646"
        },
        "body": "Now for the unexpected and out of the box proposal that sidesteps the problem entirely. Have you heard the good word about [MetaLink](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metalink)?\r\n\r\nThis is a bit like torrents, but designed as a download accelerator mechanism. You get rather high reliability of your download, since it includes chunkwise hashing. You can download individual chunks in parallel from various geodistributed CDNs (forgejo probably does not need this). You can detect corruption and partial downloads and resume arbitrarily anywhere since most of the content you already downloaded fits into a chunk that can be verified to be intact.\r\n\r\nYou could serve MetaLink to supporting clients -- mirrorbrain / mirrorcache do this -- and I suppose you could store them directly rather than serving via a CDN. This provides dubious security benefits as a free side effect of excellent corruption detection and resume support that the user doing the download doesn't have to think about."
      },
