      "detail": {
        "type": "comment",
        "comment": {
          "forge": "forgejo",
          "instance": "codeberg.org",
          "thread": {
            "forge": "forgejo",
            "instance": "codeberg.org",
            "repo": {
              "forge": "forgejo",
              "instance": "codeberg.org",
              "owner": "forgejo",
              "name": "forgejo",
              "externalId": "73144"
            },
            "kind": "issue",
            "number": "14178"
          },
          "id": "28372268"
        },
        "body": "@crueter wrote in https://codeberg.org/forgejo/forgejo/issues/14178#issuecomment-28371422:\r\n\r\n> The fundamental tenet of security is to trust nobody. ;)\r\n\r\nThis is a suitably vague statement that elides a definition of trust and also one of security. One cannot solve the fundamental tenets of security through the mechanism of picking arbitrary technological algorithms out of a lottery, then finding a way to build a feature that \"uses that algorithm\" (in this case the winning lottery entry was \"sha256\").\r\n\r\nSo we're back to the original question, what is the *goal* of installing a digest functionality into the downloads page? Without a goal we don't know what direction we're heading and certainly cannot make any progress.\r\n\r\nThe original proposal basically suggested the goal is to detect when, due to a network interruption, the file was truncated / download aborted / some other form of accidental corruption. But download clients tend to be pretty good at catching that already. Would it suffice if forgejo explicitly provided digests that disavow all uses other than corruption detection? Let us say:\r\n\r\n> THE CRC32 DIGEST IS PROVIDED “AS IS”, WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR OR INDEED ANY PURPOSE, AND SECURITY. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE UPLOADERS OR SUPPLIERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE DIGEST OR TRUST THAT THE DIGEST MEANS ANYTHING OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE DIGEST.\r\n\r\nAnd anyone who assumes that a crc32 digest is intended to be a security mechanism will have had numerous warnings signs before they got that far.\r\n\r\nThis is \"good enough\" for corruption detection.\r\n\r\n...\r\n\r\nIf the intent of the digest is for security, then we return to the wisdom of \"trust nobody\" and especially do not trust the forge itself. This is the reason why, for security reasons, I refuse to trust digests on GitHub and would likewise refuse to trust them on forgejo.\r\n\r\nIf we disregard \"trust nobody\" and decide to trust somebody, namely, the forge, but we are worried about the forge using a non-trustworthy CDN, then I would worry the html page and/or the JavaScript may be manipulated by the CDN to return a variety of suspicious things including a malicious checksum too. If that CDN is cloudflare I'm *extra* worried.\r\n\r\nSo...\r\n\r\n@eschwartz wrote in https://codeberg.org/forgejo/forgejo/issues/14178#issuecomment-22908721:\r\n\r\n> It sounds like the major reason to add it is \"for feature parity with github since github offers them\" (and \"adding it causes no harm\"), but... is that really a reason?\r\n\r\n"
      },
